{
 'title': 'Lithium: the metal behind the battery', 'context': 'A lecture in an earth resources course on where lithium comes from and how it is produced.',
 'vocab': ['brine', 'evaporation pond', 'spodumene', 'salt flat', 'extraction'],
 'script': [
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Good afternoon. Today we're turning to a metal that, until quite recently, most people had never thought about, but which is now at the centre of the global shift towards electric vehicles and renewable energy. I'm talking, of course, about lithium."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Let's start with some basic chemistry. Lithium was identified in 1817 by the Swedish chemist Johan August Arfwedson, who found it while analysing a mineral called petalite. It's the lightest of all metals, and it's so reactive that in the laboratory it has to be stored in oil to stop it reacting with air and moisture. Its lightness, and the ease with which it gives up electrons, are exactly what make it so valuable in rechargeable batteries."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "For most of the twentieth century, lithium had fairly modest uses. It was added to glass and ceramics to make them more resistant to heat, and to grease used in engines and machinery. It also has an important medical use: lithium salts have been used since the middle of the century to treat bipolar disorder. But today, the majority of lithium produced goes into batteries, and demand has grown enormously."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "So where does it come from? There are two main sources. The first is brine, which is very salty water found beneath salt flats in dry, high-altitude regions. The best-known area is the so-called lithium triangle, where Chile, Argentina and Bolivia meet, which is thought to hold more than half of the world's known lithium resources. Here, the brine is pumped up from underground into huge shallow ponds, and the sun does most of the work. As the water evaporates, the brine becomes more and more concentrated. This takes a long time, often more than a year, and then the concentrated liquid is processed into lithium carbonate."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "This method is relatively cheap, but it's controversial. The Atacama region in Chile is one of the driest places on Earth, and local Indigenous communities have raised concerns that pumping brine, along with the fresh water used in processing, may be lowering water levels and harming the lagoons where flamingos feed. The scientific evidence is still being debated, but it's clearly an issue that companies can't ignore."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "The second source is hard rock. Here, lithium is extracted from minerals, mainly one called spodumene, which is dug out of open-pit mines. Australia is the world's largest producer by this method, and it has also been the world's largest producer of lithium overall in recent years. The rock is crushed and heated to very high temperatures, and much of it has traditionally been shipped to China for refining. Hard-rock mining is faster than evaporation, but it uses more energy and produces more carbon emissions."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Because of these drawbacks, researchers and companies are investing in what's called direct lithium extraction. Instead of waiting for the sun to evaporate the water, these technologies use special filters or chemicals to pull the lithium out of the brine in hours or days, and then the remaining water can be returned underground. Several projects are also testing whether lithium can be recovered from the hot, salty water used in geothermal power plants, for example in California and in Cornwall in south-west England, where the hot springs deep underground turn out to be rich in lithium."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Finally, there's recycling. At the moment, only a small proportion of lithium from used batteries is recovered, partly because batteries are complex and difficult to take apart safely. But as the first generation of electric cars reaches the end of its life, the amount of material available for recycling will increase dramatically, and many experts believe that recycled lithium could eventually meet a significant share of demand. In the next session, we'll look at another battery metal, cobalt, and the ethical questions surrounding its supply."),
 ],
 'groups': [
  G('Questions 31–40. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.', [
   Q('note', 'Lithium was first identified in a mineral called __________', 'petalite', 'while analysing a mineral called petalite', 'It was found in petalite.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'In the laboratory, lithium is kept in __________ because it is so reactive', 'oil', 'it has to be stored in oil', 'It is stored in oil.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Older uses: added to glass, ceramics and engine __________', 'grease', 'and to grease used in engines and machinery', 'It was added to grease.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Brine is very salty water found beneath salt __________', 'flats', 'found beneath salt flats', 'Brine lies under salt flats.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'In evaporation ponds, the __________ does most of the work', 'sun', 'the sun does most of the work', 'The sun evaporates the water.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Concerns that lagoons used by __________ may be harmed', 'flamingos', 'harming the lagoons where flamingos feed', 'Flamingos feed in the lagoons.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Hard-rock lithium comes mainly from a mineral called __________', 'spodumene', 'mainly one called spodumene', 'Spodumene is the main mineral.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Much hard-rock material is sent to __________ for refining', 'China', 'shipped to China for refining', 'It is refined in China.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Direct extraction uses special __________ or chemicals', 'filters', 'these technologies use special filters or chemicals', 'Filters remove the lithium.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Geothermal lithium is being tested in California and __________', 'Cornwall', 'for example in California and in Cornwall', 'Cornwall is a test site.', max=1),
  ]),
 ],
},
